body:
@scgm0 yeah the Wayland backend has a few extra abstractions for various reasons. We basically have a custom "driver" (like `gl_manager` and the vulkan rendering context device driver) but for Wayland stuff that runs in its own thread and has a separate, simpler state.

I think the call you're looking for is `WaylandThread::window_destroy`, which should work fine paired with the graphic driver calls.

I have currently limited programming capacity due to what I assume is a burn-out, but if you want I can take a shot at this as soon as I find the chance.

All right, I just gave a look and apparently the thing simply crashed due to a few missing checks.

Here's a patch that fixes it for me:

<details>

<summary>Patch</summary>

```diff
diff --git a/platform/linuxbsd/wayland/display_server_wayland.cpp b/platform/linuxbsd/wayland/display_server_wayland.cpp
index 421c16f67a..a3417c5ef0 100644
--- a/platform/linuxbsd/wayland/display_server_wayland.cpp
+++ b/platform/linuxbsd/wayland/display_server_wayland.cpp
@@ -1279,6 +1279,10 @@ bool DisplayServerWayland::window_is_focused(WindowID p_window_id) const {
 }
 
 bool DisplayServerWayland::window_can_draw(DisplayServer::WindowID p_window_id) const {
+	if (!windows.has(p_window_id)) {
+		return false;
+	}
+
 	MutexLock mutex_lock(wayland_thread.mutex);
 
 	uint64_t last_frame_time = wayland_thread.window_get_last_frame_time(p_window_id);
@@ -1374,6 +1378,10 @@ void DisplayServerWayland::window_set_vsync_mode(DisplayServer::VSyncMode p_vsyn
 }
 
 DisplayServer::VSyncMode DisplayServerWayland::window_get_vsync_mode(DisplayServer::WindowID p_window_id) const {
+	if (!windows.has(p_window_id)) {
+		return DisplayServer::VSYNC_DISABLED;
+	}
+
 	const WindowData &wd = windows[p_window_id];
 	if (wd.emulate_vsync) {
 		return DisplayServer::VSYNC_ENABLED;
@@ -1391,7 +1399,7 @@ DisplayServer::VSyncMode DisplayServerWayland::window_get_vsync_mode(DisplayServ
 	}
 #endif // GLES3_ENABLED
 
-	return DisplayServer::VSYNC_ENABLED;
+	return DisplayServer::VSYNC_DISABLED;
 }
 
 void DisplayServerWayland::window_start_drag(WindowID p_window) {
diff --git a/scene/main/window.cpp b/scene/main/window.cpp
index c210d85dbf..beb565d9dd 100644
--- a/scene/main/window.cpp
+++ b/scene/main/window.cpp
@@ -954,7 +954,7 @@ void Window::set_visible(bool p_visible) {
 	}
 
 	String display_name = DisplayServer::get_singleton()->get_name();
-	if (display_name != "Windows" && display_name != "X11") {
+	if (display_name != "Windows" && display_name != "X11" && display_name != "Wayland") {
 		ERR_FAIL_NULL_MSG(get_parent(), "Can't change visibility of main window.");
 	}

```

</details>

This patch also changes the fallback value of the vsync query to `DISABLED` since it's what the other platforms and driver do.

That said, while I approve of the concept, I'm afraid that this specific approach might cause some issues. "Hiding" a window currently deletes it outright, which means that when a new window is created it will actually make a new one through `create_sub_window`. This method does not take in an ID (and I don't think it should), instead returning the last id + 1. This means that the constant `MAIN_WINDOW_ID` will point to nowhere all the time and most `DisplayServer` methods which provide a default `MAIN_WINDOW_ID` argument will error out instead. Probably a more thorough approach would be to implement some visibility logic (e.g. `window_map()` `window_unmap()` methods).

You can see this issue with the following script, attached to a `Control` node:

<details>
<summary>Script</summary>

```gdscript
extends Control

func show_window():
	get_window().show()
	print(get_window().get_window_id())
	DisplayServer.window_get_size()

func _input(what):
	if what is InputEventKey:
		if what.keycode == KEY_H:
			print(get_window().get_window_id())
			get_window().hide()
			get_tree().create_timer(2).timeout.connect(show_window)

```

</details>

Press "h", it will print `0`, then `1` and give an error on `window_get_size()`.

This would definitely warrant some discussion in a proposal though, is there already one?
